      "rows": [
        {
          "dimension": "Smart-contract / oracle",
          "level": "Medium",
          "note": "A Chaos Labs automated oracle cap update on wstETH briefly underpriced collateral and triggered roughly $21M in wrongful liquidations, demonstrating that automated risk tooling can itself introduce systemic errors.",
          "source": ""
        },
        {
          "dimension": "Centralization",
          "level": "Medium",
          "note": "Protocol risk management has historically depended on one or two external firms (Gauntlet, Chaos Labs); Gauntlet's unilateral exit in 2024 left a temporary gap in the Risk Steward role, concentrating operational control dangerously.",
          "source": ""
        },
        {
          "dimension": "Liquidity",
          "level": "Medium",
          "note": "The Egorov CRV position illustrated how a single large borrower can create a tail-risk scenario where forced liquidation of illiquid collateral could cause cascading bad debt across the protocol.",
          "source": ""
        },
        {
          "dimension": "Regulatory",
          "level": "Low",
          "note": "A US Government address withdrew $5.4M from Aave, signaling government actors are active users of the protocol but no enforcement action has targeted Aave directly as of mid-2026.",
